{
 "what": "What a device-access check reads on a Linux bench, in the three places the bench tier runs: directly on the host, and in tools/bench_in_container.py's container with the device groups kept and with them withheld. For each, the USB devices under /sys/bus/usb/devices with the attributes discovery reads (idVendor, idProduct, busnum, devnum, serial) and the ttys under each interface; os.stat and os.access(R_OK | W_OK) of the USB nodes, the probe's tty, its /dev/serial/by-id link and /dev/ttyS0 where there is one; and this process's credentials, with the groups of this login (os.getgroups) beside the account's groups in the account database (os.getgrouplist). The unit tier replays these rather than typing a mode, an owner or a group.",
 "recorded_on": "2026-09-27",
 "how": "A throwaway pytest module in tests/bench, run once with the tier's markers: on the host with AGENTIC_HIL_BENCH=1, then through tools/bench_in_container.py and tools/bench_in_container.py --without-device-group (rootless Podman, the tier's Debian 13 image). The host is a stock Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS (kernel 6.8) with OpenOCD's udev rules installed and one Nucleo-F446RE attached through its on-board ST-Link V2-1 (0483:374b). The module opened nothing. The probe serial is replaced by the suite's synthetic serial 066BFF505050505050505050, the account's own name and its private group by [withheld], and the one USB device that is neither the probe nor a root hub is left out; nothing else is changed.",
